ut_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"0.5249246213812131"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":11},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## R² (Coefficient of Determination)\n\n$$R^2 = 1 - \\frac{SS_{res}}{SS_{tot}}$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$SS_{res} = \\Sigma(y_i - \\hat{y}_i)^2$$\n$$SS_{tot} = \\Sigma(y_i - \\bar{y})^2$$\n\n$SS_{res}$ is the sum of squares of residuals (errors) (variability in data the model cannot explain)\n$SS_{tot}$ is the total sum of squares which measures the variance of the observed values around their mean\n\nThe fraction itself would be the percentage of variability the model cannot explain. So 1 minus that is the variability it can explain.\n\nR² basically tells us how much (in percent) of the variance is explained by the model. We could consider this to be the accuracy metric of regression.\n\n* Easy to interpret as percentage of explained variance\n* R² can be artificially inflated with useless variables\n    * Adding new variables will always either do nothing or explain more\n* Does not really tell us if the model is good or bad, model can have high R² but still large residuals\n    * Explains most of the variance, but predictions still far off in absolute terms\n* More suited for linear models (not so much for random forests, neural networks etc.)","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"y_pred = reg.predict(X_test)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-05-27T10:05:39.475411Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-05-27T10:05:39.475770Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-05-27T10:05:39.481603Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-05-27T10:05:39.475742Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-05-27T10:05:39.480588Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":12},{"cell_type":"code","source":"SSR_reg = sum((y_test-y_pred)**2)\nSST_reg = sum((y_test-np.mean(y_test))**2)\n\nR2_reg = 1-(SSR_reg/SST_reg)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-05-27T10:05:39.482716Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-05-27T10:05:39.482959Z","iop